README.md:13:The cluster stage persists every profiled entry in a stable cluster identity. Strong judged links at or above 0.80 form broad components. Components with at least six current embeddings are recursively subdivided only when a semantic split improves embedding fit and judged pairs support separation or a sparse bridge connects distinct centroids. Each subdivided component is persisted as a parent cluster group containing its child clusters; domain-based groups remain many-to-many topic proposals. Judged cross-cluster links at or above 0.65 remain separate neighbor edges. Embedding centroids provide explicitly labeled suggestions when no judged link is available. The projection retains child cluster and parent group identities by member overlap across rebuilds and stores membership, multiple neighbors, topic groups, and generic arrangement metadata. Profile domains are normalized before grouping, and very broad domain groups are excluded. Signals are a separately versioned derivation: deterministic cited profile facts, dated or numbered saved-note titles, and media types are merged with cited layout facts extracted from selected source passages for members of formed clusters. A note title's date is marked as a record date; publication dates do not count as event dates. This stage does not invalidate a saved profile or embedding. A multi-entry cluster uses its anchor entry title until a model annotation supplies a descriptive label and summary; a changed member profile clears that annotation for review. `mgraph status` reports annotated and pending multi-entry counts. Candidate pair retrieval routes through nearest persisted cluster centroids as an additional source while retaining global vector, lexical, concept, and explicit-link discovery.
