README.md:37:`zsh run_parallel_profiles.sh` applies the pilot migration, screens fetched web identities, repairs saved primary names, refreshes stale Kimi judgments, and runs disjoint Kimi profile shards. It then repairs names again, audits every eligible current-version profile, runs disjoint Cloudflare embedding shards, refreshes judgments invalidated by new profiles, and runs disjoint Kimi relation shards up to 20,000 distinct pairs. Finally it persists clusters and neighbors, analyzes cited layout signals for multi-entry clusters in disjoint shards, rebuilds the graph, and annotates all multi-entry clusters lacking a current summary in disjoint shards. The rolling embedding runner refreshes the graph and annotates up to five clusters per annotation shard on each pass while profiling continues; `MGRAPH_ROLLING_ANNOTATE_LIMIT` adjusts the per-shard batch. Defaults are eight refresh, sixteen profile, four embedding, eight relation, eight signal, four final annotation, and four rolling annotation workers; `MGRAPH_REFRESH_WORKERS`, `MGRAPH_PROFILE_WORKERS`, `MGRAPH_EMBED_WORKERS`, `MGRAPH_RELATION_WORKERS`, `MGRAPH_SIGNAL_WORKERS`, `MGRAPH_ANNOTATE_WORKERS`, and `MGRAPH_ROLLING_ANNOTATE_WORKERS` set each stage independently. The audit stops downstream inference if any eligible profile is missing, stale, or has unsupported quotes, or if a web page has not been screened. The script resumes from versioned persisted projections. Kimi K3 profiling and identity screening default to `low` reasoning effort; set `MGRAPH_DO_PROFILE_REASONING_EFFORT` or `MGRAPH_DO_IDENTITY_REASONING_EFFORT` in `.env` to evaluate another effort level. Pair judgments use the model default unless `MGRAPH_DO_REASONING_EFFORT` is set. Run `.venv/bin/python -m unittest discover -s tests -v`, inspect `mgraph status`, and manually compare sampled claims and property quotes with `read_memory` before restarting a large run after a profile change.
README.md:41:The overview offers three layouts of the same persisted multi-entry clusters. `Clusters` lists every cluster as a card carrying its label, node count, layout arrangement, broadest shared domain, and summary, ordered by relationship neighbourhood so linked clusters stay contiguous and the grid has no holes. `Map` lays the same cards along a strip that only scrolls sideways (a vertical mouse wheel scrolls it too) as an archipelago of islands, each a group of related clusters separated from the next by open space. The graph API returns each visible cluster's five nearest visible clusters by embedding centroid as `affinity`, used only for layout. Clusters joined by a drawn relationship always share an island; islands then merge by average relatedness (centroid affinity, lifted by relationships and nudged by a shared profile domain) while they stay small enough to read, and clusters close to nothing gather in a final `Unsorted` island. Islands are named by their majority domain or the rarest label words their clusters share, and the most related islands sit side by side. Inside each island the cards are scattered organically, pulled together along relationships and strong affinity, with bigger clusters drawn toward the middle. Opening a cluster expands it in place: its card rises to the top of the strip, and its nodes unroll to its right in the cluster's own layout, still only growing sideways: timelines and sequences run along a horizontal spine with month chapters or step numbers and cards alternating above and below, galleries become image tiles, place layouts become one column group per cited place, and connected clusters place linked nodes side by side with their links drawn. Measured neighbours and then the rest of its island stack beneath it and in a rail after the nodes, and the rest of the strip dims and slides right to make room. Clicking the open card again, pressing Escape, or clicking empty space collapses it and scrolls the strip back to where it was before the first cluster was opened, even after stepping through related clusters. `Constellation` swaps the set into a force-directed canvas where every cluster stays labelled and the settled layout is scaled to fit rather than clamped to the frame. Solid lines are measured relationships, dashed lines embedding suggestions. The collection picker filters by persisted cluster group or subdivided parent group. Single-entry clusters remain internal records and do not appear in any view. A header toggle switches between system, light, and dark themes; the choice persists.
README.md:43:Opening a card from the Clusters or Constellation views (or choosing `Open full view` inside the Map expansion) goes straight to the cluster's full page. Its nodes occupy the centre, ranked neighbouring clusters alternate into rails on both sides showing relationship basis, score, evidence-pair count and summary, and selecting one re-centres the view and extends a traversal trail you can step back through. Group chips return to the overview filtered by that group. The centre renders in the shape the cluster's layout signals chose: timeline entries become dated chapters on a spine ordered by the persisted entry order, sequences become numbered steps, galleries become media tiles captioned with extracted concepts, place layouts group by cited place, and connected clusters list the judged links between their own members. Place layouts show cited place labels, not geographic coordinates; a true map needs coordinate resolution. Entries with unknown date or order are listed separately rather than guessed at. Global search still covers every projected entry, including entries without a profile or graph position. The saved graph updates through `refresh-graph`; rolling embeddings refresh it during a live backfill.
